#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134811} :activity/id #uuid "1faaba32-288c-58f7-9e8b-36ad43309761" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135102 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ exist, then the **divergence** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is the scalar given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as \n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the dot product\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\, \\mathbf i + e^{yx} \\, \\mathbf j .\n$$\n\nThen, we can calculate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the dot product formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =(i\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}+j\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y})\\cdot (2xy\\,i+{e}^{yx}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yx}) \\\\  & =2y+x{e}^{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "086f12c3-0ab5-54ed-8b4e-5c70e8c7c7e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5116 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5c2a161a-d267-5999-8795-ba9baba0146f" :step/math-academy-id 32111 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424229}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "5d2f80d4-10e2-5fdc-b52f-7b5cadac99d2" :step/math-academy-id 32553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"} :step/id #uuid "eb39b82a-9c43-56ff-9c10-70ad9f735fab" :step/math-academy-id 33322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135114 :tutorial/content "Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the divergence of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ and the point $S,$ shown below. Imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\n![](images/a7/a785c84976242d699af11ab2203600e2007e95e35b60665ba9882c8f0f25bce3.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $S$ acts like a **source**. You can think of this as an open faucet, pushing water onto the plane and away from itself. \n\nSince $S$ acts like a source, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is nonzero. By convention, if a point $S$ acts like a source, the divergence is positive:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0\n$$\n\nIn this particular case, *every* point in our vector field acts as a source. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$ everywhere.\n\nIf we reverse the situation, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b4/b4c923bbde36568e4e2f36f7d388622a520d0799d558049f72d0194e0bd679f7.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $T$ acts like a **sink**. You can think of this as an open plughole that sucks in the surrounding water.\n\nSince $T$ acts like a sink, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is also nonzero. By convention, if a point $T$ acts like a sink, the divergence is negative:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0\n$$\n\nEvery point in our vector field acts like a sink in this case. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$ everywhere.\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of some vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **solenoidal.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2cdf73b-116c-5cf4-a5a2-a8c95e447127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5518 :tutorial/title "Physical Interpretation of Divergence"} :step/id #uuid "6a9298c4-106a-569c-bd0b-5d24d3e71b0f" :step/math-academy-id 34359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "77b5a013-eab6-50c3-9f63-2a51f2e967b2" :step/math-academy-id 35525 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135120 :tutorial/content "The following two vector fields behave either as a source or a sink. Therefore, they both have divergence.\n\n![](images/93/939efa85ebfe4805a27595b60a797b8a3435075590ee77f7a18d04bd76d91f1a.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are solenoidal (i.e., they each have zero divergence).\n\n![](images/ed/edb2f908fa78439bf985b5754c9fcc5968c21926dbe124297b67af046a9f70a3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ddbe7ae2-caaf-5436-b1c7-aabc0eec115d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5793 :tutorial/title "Examples of Vector Field With Divergence and Solenoidal Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "88b27d23-7358-51e8-9457-a8b063ed5a52" :step/math-academy-id 35536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135121 :tutorial/content "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "9b8de083-51c2-581b-ad36-a773dca8af05" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5794 :tutorial/title "Some Important Notes About the Divergence of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "e856caa2-317e-5779-b004-597af558a506" :step/math-academy-id 35537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134822}}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134010}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407634} :activity/id #uuid "23bb149c-27d7-5070-88b1-a3124f094ad2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} {:answer/id #uuid "30bd69d5-9aed-5f53-bd57-88c0d8b71e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407615} {:answer/id #uuid "951b5e1a-06b7-5f54-b81b-2e7935528e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2537554-7ead-5776-b7ea-b0a5c6ea2d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407622} {:answer/id #uuid "de576d13-a320-57ea-81fe-65cb1f522e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f12593-79dd-5e1b-8574-a974a94bfc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f48c368c-417f-5ffe-9791-056b549ac545" :question/math-academy-id "q-105559" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d73206f-cfb2-5389-b819-954a52ec5c0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} {:answer/id #uuid "941fb884-9dbd-5e6e-bdb3-9c13326a2f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9966256-2b26-5637-969d-ec652f55ff58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0916bb-4bf2-5bfb-8625-f74c2a28d4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407626} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb625e-fb38-58c5-8c7d-425f0374e2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} :answer-field/id #uuid "fafbd1ee-fb02-5785-b3f4-4426b308c48e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db7680f4-57f3-5a1a-a81a-52c92b75f829" :question/math-academy-id "q-105555" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43f42226-1025-58c1-878e-7c4041f020a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e74b99-ee56-54e9-ae3f-42d5ee7c0a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407613} {:answer/id #uuid "5070438f-bcf7-51c2-8463-01a5c462b9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407616} {:answer/id #uuid "67f8c8f5-44cd-5c0e-a53a-65de21b3fa5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407617} {:answer/id #uuid "803f938a-69cc-5cc9-9644-a563d2a7cf5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407619} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b2e55e-a370-570d-ba5a-e8f0f307eabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407613} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ff6b8b-be06-5d38-bc53-8c8fbff6866c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2847820b-e13e-563d-bfbb-bab640514e5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105572" :question/problem "![](images/58/584016c88e5916984e181118d52caab95dba1fd0d861e0fd248a4e023d1b07da.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "84dd7364-713d-5340-9f9c-2da553617182"}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407612}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436937} :activity/id #uuid "b0b7ba59-d2e5-5c0e-aa1c-0c290b8b930c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424206} {:answer/id #uuid "53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424207} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} {:answer/id #uuid "c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424217} {:answer/id #uuid "cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/math-academy-id "q-131195" :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0c687dc8-83c3-51c4-b6a7-2402b933a9ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "627918cc-4b21-5f54-b208-cb86d8a90fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238922} {:answer/id #uuid "9f41d10b-f851-5d45-9827-61f7b022fc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239445} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e51c7e-db29-5f2f-8366-bcfa0cafd1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239898} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb49dbb-7200-53b3-81d0-14d6d6fcc22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239981} {:answer/id #uuid "e21e0841-2d32-541f-bd4e-00d6bca599f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239445} :answer-field/id #uuid "116238d5-19f2-50cb-9748-dd30cea9d39a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2172851-a396-51c3-bd4e-4e1278b37143" :question/math-academy-id "q-105575" :question/problem "![](images/11/11d38951b884f17616a61403d793322d0e2ea28910e806fe8f018251508352a1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0eb2dca7-e256-5a91-aeab-a6e0be87d07f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a914c9-ac5b-510b-8770-498ebbd342d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436921} {:answer/id #uuid "88e03628-9e8e-5879-9ede-a96902f3797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07bd93-8453-5012-b970-faf3a1a28f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436927} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d3d9a8-1c50-5ad8-967c-67a2b8bdc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xyz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436928} {:answer/id #uuid "d674ed52-351d-5f4a-9c10-e0caa107251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e44755-6b9a-5cca-b1ed-0c837e17c6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22c40f40-e4e5-529d-a83f-3f46f140c8db" :question/math-academy-id "q-105557" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{y}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,j+y{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nFind $\\text{div}\\,F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\  & =3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a7d78a0-8d6d-590f-8d8a-84c35fdbd7e4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ab80cc-c857-5dfb-9a3a-cfdfd3d6fc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436922} {:answer/id #uuid "335bd84d-490e-5a69-979b-a11b772a9908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436923} {:answer/id #uuid "36f39977-9420-5792-a25e-40ed825c033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} {:answer/id #uuid "78e9e533-d5f7-5054-82d6-9d333edf1c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(1+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436925} {:answer/id #uuid "af0cee1b-58b3-5b9b-a7e7-5c521761b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(2-y-z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3222d-f787-5312-b008-17567e5ae0c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36837fe3-6724-5929-9249-1bb3cf0a5e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131175" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={e}^{2x}\\,i-y{e}^{2x}\\,j-z{e}^{2x}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-z{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af33c61a-5271-559b-9054-88db8d041045" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answ